# Consult external docs (Cursor & Discord) as source of truth

When the task involves Cursor internals (MCP config, rules, skills, agents,
cloud agents, hooks) or the Discord API (bot auth, gateway, REST endpoints,
voice, SDKs), **query the Context7 MCP before writing or changing code.**

## Why

Cursor and Discord ship frequent breaking changes. Model memory goes stale.
The Context7 MCP returns the current published docs with working snippets, so
it should be treated as the source of truth over recalled knowledge.

## How

1. Resolve the library ID first, then query:
   - Cursor docs → library ID `/websites/cursor`
   - Discord API docs → library ID `/discord/discord-api-docs`
   - Discord developer platform → library ID `/llmstxt/discord_llms_txt`
2. Ask a specific question ("How to authenticate a bot and connect to the
   gateway"), not a bare keyword ("auth").
3. Cite the source URL Context7 returns in your explanation to the user.
4. If Context7 returns nothing relevant, say so plainly and fall back to
   your own knowledge — do not fabricate docs.

## Limits

- Do not call the docs MCP more than 3 times per question; reuse prior results.
- Do not include secrets (API keys, tokens, credentials) in the query text.
- Treat retrieved docs as context enrichment, not a guarantee — verify
  against the live docs page when a detail is load-bearing.
